working on Packer.

00:00:16.274 --> 00:00:17.439
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: All right.

00:00:17.439 --> 00:00:19.630
This is the third video
in a series covering open

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:22.090
source tools from HashiCorp
and how we can use them

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:23.710
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.390
In the last video,
you learned how

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:28.090
developers can create repeatable
development environments

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:29.170
with Vagrant.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:32.800
In this video, Matt and I
are going to cover Packer.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.745
Matt, can give us an
overview of Packer?

00:00:34.745 --> 00:00:35.040
MATT HOOKER: Yeah.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:35.740
Thanks, Kelsey.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:36.887
I'd be happy to.

00:00:36.887 --> 00:00:39.220
Packer may at first seem a
lot like Vagrant in the sense

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.200
that it can be used
to define the software

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:42.790
and configuration for a VM.

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:45.200
But unlike Vagrant, the
artifact it produces

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.970
is a machine image, as
opposed to a running VM.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.680
The machine image can be used
to create VM's preconfigured

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.230
exactly how you want them all
through a simple Packer config

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:54.385
file.

00:00:54.385 --> 00:00:55.510
This is where it gets cool.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:58.180
With a single configuration
and common specification,

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.130
Packer can produce
almost any machine image

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.350
like Google Compute Engine,
other cloud providers,

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:03.520
and even Docker images.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:04.478
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah.

00:01:04.478 --> 00:01:05.620
Packer is pretty dope.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.630
I've used it in quite
a few of my projects,

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.580
and know a few
people using Packer.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.860
In fact, I'm the original author
of the GCE builder for Packer,

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:14.170
going back many years now.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:15.385
MATT HOOKER: Nice,
I didn't know that.

00:01:15.385 --> 00:01:15.885
OK.

00:01:15.885 --> 00:01:18.100
Then you might have some
kind of demo or something

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:18.710
you could show us.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:19.090
Right?

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:20.756
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
That's why we're here.

00:01:20.756 --> 00:01:22.300
Continuing the story
from last time,

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.758
we're going to create a GCE
image based on a similar config

00:01:24.758 --> 00:01:26.020
you saw in the Vagrant video.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.770
You'll recall from
that video that Vagrant

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.910
was to be used by developers,
but the main goal of creating

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:35.160
their application and sharing
across a Vagrant VM on Google.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.450
Now we're going to start
sliding the perspective

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:39.910
from our developer view
over to our operations

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:43.350
view where you want to
deploy these images on GCE

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.770
for the running the
app for our users.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:47.680
So the image we're
going to create

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.560
will have our '80s
style arcade game,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.060
and its dependencies
installed and configured

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.560
to start at boot time.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.590
Matt, how do we get started?

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:57.910
MATT HOOKER: Let's do it.

00:01:57.910 --> 00:01:59.910
You want to start by
going to Packer.io

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:02.540
and downloading the version
appropriate for your platform.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.494
In the last video, you saw how
to set up your GCP project,

00:02:05.494 --> 00:02:06.910
so we'll skip that
part this time,

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:09.759
but I think this time we're
going to use a GC instance.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:10.740
Right, Kelsey?

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:11.740
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Right.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.390
I have a machine called DEV
that we're going to use.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.350
We're going to be using Packer
to create a new GCE operating

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:18.700
system image.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.340
I'll first show you what
we currently only have,

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:25.100
the standard GCE
images available to us.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:25.950
MATT HOOKER: Great.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.287
Now let's flip over to our
terminal on the DEV instance

00:02:28.287 --> 00:02:29.870
where I already have
Packer installed,

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:31.927
and my Packer config
file ready to go.

00:02:31.927 --> 00:02:33.510
Let's kick off the
image field process

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.140
now with the Packard
build command.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:37.640
OK.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.140
Now that we've got
that going, let's

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:42.650
take a look at the hyperspace
JSON config file we're using.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:45.220
Packer config files
have two primary parts.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:46.850
A section that
specifies builders

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:48.902
and another for provisioners.

00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:51.110
In our builder section, you
can see that I'm not only

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.570
defining the variables needed
to produce the Google Compute

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:55.670
Engine image, I'm
specifying attributes

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:57.230
such as the base
operating system

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:58.940
and the name of the new image.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.654
Let's take a quick look at
the provisioner section.

00:03:01.654 --> 00:03:03.320
Packer allows you to
customize the image

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.690
with any number of
provisioner types,

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:09.647
like basic shell scripts,
which is what you see here,

00:03:09.647 --> 00:03:11.480
or even using other
configuration management

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:15.894
tools such as Chef,
Puppet, Ansible, et cetera.

00:03:15.894 --> 00:03:17.310
But back to the
topic of builders.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.060
Kelsey, do you want
to describe how Packer

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.490
builds an image for a GCP?

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:21.573
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: You bet.

00:03:21.573 --> 00:03:23.160
First, Packer spins
up a new instance

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.050
with the base image
that was described.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.900
Then, Packer will
SSH into the new VM,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.700
and apply any
configuration defined

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.310
in the provisionary section
of the Packer config.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:35.010
Here, we're basically
reusing the shell script

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.230
you saw on the Vagrant
video, but split it out

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:38.030
into its own file.

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:43.180
Let's see how the
build process is doing.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:43.797
OK.

00:03:43.797 --> 00:03:46.130
Looks like it's running through
those provisionary steps

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:47.370
and our shell scripts.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:49.310
We'll skip ahead
here a little bit,

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:51.179
even though I know
we all love watching

00:03:51.179 --> 00:03:52.220
deadman packages install.

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:56.960
Once the provision and
customizations have finished,

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.650
Packer will terminate
the running GCE instance,

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:01.910
but will leave behind
the Persistent Disk,

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.460
or PD as we like to call it.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:06.950
The PD contains the
base operating system,

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:09.650
and our customizations,
and is used in a template

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:12.100
to create the new GCE image.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.230
Once the image is created,
Packer deletes the PD,

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:19.245
so there aren't any leftover
resources in your GCP project.

00:04:19.245 --> 00:04:20.370
MATT HOOKER: It's finished.

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:22.770
Let's take a look at
the list of images now.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:25.130
And sure enough, there's
our new hyperspace image.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:27.260
We can even quickly spin
up a new GC instance

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:28.730
with our brand new image.

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:30.860
I'll click the checkbox
next to the image,

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:32.550
and click the Create
Instance button.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.330
I'll keep all the defaults, but
since hyperspace is a web app,

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:38.130
I'll enable the HTTP firewall
rule for the instance,

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:39.170
and then click Create.

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:40.336
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Hey, Matt.

00:04:40.336 --> 00:04:42.630
While we're waiting for this
new instance to come up,

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:46.580
let's take a look at the
console output as it's booting.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:51.130
